  "raw_text": "68\n\n(Ex/Ej\n:\n \nAlberto\n \nManagua\n \nra diman naiwa\n \n–\n \nAlberto arrived in\n \nManagua\n \ntoday\n \n/ Alberto llegó hoy a\n \nManagua\n)\n\nDimaika\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nentrance\n, entryway,\n \ngate,\n \ndress\n \n/ entrada, puerta,\n \nvestido\n\nDimi balaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto come in\n \n/ entrar\n\nDimi yukuwaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto go in and hide\n \n/ entrar y esconderse\n\n(\nEx/Ej\n(\nlfb32\n):\n \nSisara tintka watla ra dimi yukuwan\n \n–\n \nS\ni\ns\ne\nra went in the tent and hid\n \n/ S\ní\nsara entró en la tienda y se\n\nescondió\n)\n\nKupia dimaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto enter the heart, to reach the heart\n \n/ entrar al corazón, llegar al corazón\n\nSmalkanka ba upla kupia kat dimbia\n \n(\nth-19-tit\n)\n \n–\n \nEffort to Reach the Heart\n \n/ Llegar al corazón\n\nKwala\n \ndimaia\n \n(a/t:\n \nKuala dimaia\n) (v)\n \n–\n \nto dress oneself, to clothe oneself, to dress, to wear\n \n/ vestirse, ponerse,\nvestir\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nmy102\n):\n \nKwala pihni dimi\n \n–\n \nwearing white clothes\n \n/ vistiendo ropas blancas)\n\nLalah an dimi ba\n \n–\n \nthe money coming, income\n \n/ el dinero que viene, ingresos\n\nPât dimaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto get into trouble\n \n/ meterse en problemas\n\nPrâk dimaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto put a shirt on, to put a dress on, to put a robe on, to put clothing on\n \n/ ponerse una camisa,\nponerse un vestido, ponerse una bata, ponerse ropa\n\nSkul dimaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto enroll in school, to enter school, to go to school\n \n/ matricularse en la escuela, ingresar a la\nescuela, ir a la escuela\n\nSkul dimi wahma/tiara\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nyoung man/woman going to school\n \n/\n \nel/la\n \njoven\n \nque va\n \na\n \nla escuela\n,\n\nalumno/alumna\n\nTilara dimaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto enter among\n,\n \nto get involved in\n \n/ entrar,\n \ninmiscuirse, participar,\n \ninvolucrarse en\n\n(\nEx/Ej\n(\nmwb\n-\n12/19\n):\n \nPulitik bara war aiklabanka nani ba tilara yawan dimaia apia\n \nsa\n \n–\n \nWe must not get involved in war\n\nand politics; We must be neutral\n.\n \n/ No debemos involucrarnos en la guerra y la política; Debemos ser neutrales.\n)\n\nTras dimaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto go into debt\n \n/ endeudarse\n\n(\nEx/Ej\n(\nwqu\n-\n08/19\n):\n \nDîa muni\n \ntras dimaia apia sa?\n \n–\n \nWhy must we not get into debt?\n \n/ ¿Por qué no debemos\n\nendeudarnos?\n)\n\n(\nEx/Ej\n(\nhf\n-\n14\n):\n \nYang mayi wal dîa daukaia sip sna ki tras\n \ndimaia apia dukiara?\n \n–\n \nWhat am I and my mate able to do in order\n\nnot to go into\n \ndebt?\n \n/ ¿Qué podemos hacer yo y mi pareja para no endeudarnos?\n)\n\nYu dimaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto set (sun)\n \n/ ponerse (sol)\n\n(Ex/Ej:\n \nyu diman ningkara\n \n–\n \nafter sunset\n \n/ después de la\n \npuesta del sol\n)\n\nYu dimanka\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nsunset\n \n/ puesta de sol\n, ocaso\n\nDimwaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto sink, to go into water\n \n/ hundirse, entrar al agua, sumergirse\n\nDinar\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nnoon\n \n/ mediodía\n\nDinar pata\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nlunch\n \n/ almuerzo\n\nDingkaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto put, to get (into), to put on clothing, to submerge, to immerse, to dip\n \n/ poner, meter, vestir, vestirse,\nsumergir\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nth-5\n):\n \nBîla wala kum dingkpara, baku sin bîla kum dakbaia apia sa\n \n–\n \nDo not insert another word, likewise, we must not take\naway a word\n \n/ No inserte otra palabra, del mismo modo, no debemos quitar una palabra)\n\n(\nEx/Ej\n(\nmwb\n-\n09/19\n):\n \nWan sinska ra\n \ndîa dingki\n \nba yawan aman kaikaia sa\n \n–\n \nWe must watch carefully what is entering our mind\n \n/\n\nDebemos\n \nobservar cuidadosamente lo que está entrando en nuestra mente\n)\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nwqu\n-\n09/20\n):\n \nSturi kunin nani ba\n \ndîa muni pât ra wan dingkaia sip sa?\n \n–\n \nWhy can false stories get us into trouble?\n \n/\n \nPor qué\n\nlas historias falsas pueden meternos en problemas?\n)\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nlfb\n-\n74\n):\n \nJordan awala ra diman, bara Jan Jisas ra aiska liura dingkan\n \n–\n \nThey went into the Jordan river, and John immersed\n\nJesus entirely into the water\n \n/ Fueron al río Jordán, y Juan sumergió a Jesús por completo en el agua\n)\n\nBanhki dingkaia\n \n(a/t:\n \nUlbi dingkaia\n) (v)\n \n–\n \nto fill out and put in\n \n/ llenar y poner\n\n(Ex/Ej\n(\nmwb-12/20\n):\n \nPat luki kaikram ki King Lâka Sturka Smalki nani Skulka ra waia dukiara wauhkataya ba banhki\ndingkaia?\n \n–\n \nHave you already considered filling out and submitting the form for School for Kingdom Evangelizers?\n \n/ Ha\nconsiderado ya completar y enviar el formulario de Escuela para Evangelizadores del Reino?)\n\nDaiwan taya saika dadingkra\n \n(n-agent/s-agente)\n \n–\n \ntanner\n \n/ curtidor\n\nKwala\n \ndingkaia\n \n(a/t:\n \nKuala dingkaia\n) (v)\n \n–\n \nto dress, to clothe, to put on clothing\n \n/ vestir, vestirse\n\nMihtadingka\n \n(Mihta dingka)\n \n(\ndb\n,\ndict-alt\n,\ndictu-alt\n;\n \nMata-dingka\n-\ndict\n,\ndictu\n;\n \nMatta-dingka\n-\ndict-alt\n;\n \nMatadingka\n-\ndb-\nalt\n,\ndictu-alt\n;\n \nMatdingka\n-\ndictu-alt\n) (n/s)\n \n–\n \nring\n \n/ anillo\n\nMihta dingkaika\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nglove, mitten\n \n/ guante, manopla\n\nPint dingkaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto paint, to apply paint\n \n/ pintar, aplicar pintura",
